WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.948 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.948 --> 00:00:03.409 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:05.357 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.357 --> 00:00:12.230 align:middle line:90%


00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:13.730 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE:
Today, we're going

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:16.850 align:middle line:84%
to talk about
stochastic processes,

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.350 align:middle line:90%
the second set of lectures.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:26.240 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to begin with a
discussion of Brownian motion.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.920 align:middle line:84%
Brownian motion is a process
that I'm sure most of you

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:31.800 align:middle line:90%
have heard of.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.360 align:middle line:84%
And it was named after Robert
Brown, an English botanist.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.470 align:middle line:84%
And this is an
interesting case where

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:44.000 align:middle line:84%
a scientist observes
some dynamics in nature

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:50.390 align:middle line:84%
and then is concerned about
what mathematical model might

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.350 align:middle line:90%
describe that behavior.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:58.880 align:middle line:84%
Turns out that it was, I
guess, Albert Einstein who

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.379 align:middle line:84%
formalized this Brownian
motion model, mathematically.

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:10.880 align:middle line:84%
And Norbert Wiener,
our MIT hero--

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.730 align:middle line:84%
those of you familiar
with the math department

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:16.910 align:middle line:84%
know about the
Norbert Wiener room

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:20.870 align:middle line:84%
that we have-- and he developed
the mathematical foundation

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:22.350 align:middle line:90%
for Brownian motion.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:28.580 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's take a look at a
simulation of Brownian motion

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:29.970 align:middle line:90%
in two dimensions.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:35.970 align:middle line:84%
So this is the kind of dynamics
that Brown was observing.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:39.680 align:middle line:84%
And so in these
slides, we're going

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.750 align:middle line:84%
to start the process at the
origin of the space, where

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:49.280 align:middle line:84%
the red dot is, and then move
around in two dimensions, where

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.990 align:middle line:84%
each step is a random
move in the x direction

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:57.540 align:middle line:84%
and a random move
in the y direction.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:04.090 align:middle line:84%
And for this simulation, we
use Gaussian distributions.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:08.620 align:middle line:84%
But as I page
through these graphs,

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:13.290 align:middle line:84%
one can see how a
particle moving according

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:16.200 align:middle line:84%
to these dynamics will
start at the origin

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.260 align:middle line:90%
and evolve to the final red dot.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:26.110 align:middle line:84%
And if we hold down the
graphs and see where it goes,

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:30.960 align:middle line:84%
you can see that
basically there's

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:36.040 align:middle line:84%
purely random dynamics but
of a very special kind.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:41.610 align:middle line:84%
And so the special kind
is that the movements

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:48.780 align:middle line:84%
are jagged and unpredictable
in terms of their direction.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.960 align:middle line:84%
Something that's really quite
interesting when you just

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:56.760 align:middle line:84%
look at this and page
through the dynamics here,

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:01.600 align:middle line:84%
animating the process,
the movements, in fact,

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:08.050 align:middle line:84%
have no trend in them to
move left or up or down.

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:13.590 align:middle line:84%
And so the movements
we see basically

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:18.550 align:middle line:84%
are movements that are
symmetrical in their dynamics.

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:21.630 align:middle line:84%
And so that's a very
interesting property

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:25.690 align:middle line:84%
that we would like to
characterize mathematically.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:31.770 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at how we
define Brownian motion in one

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:33.360 align:middle line:90%
dimension.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:40.560 align:middle line:84%
So in one dimension, we are
simply looking at a graph.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.035 align:middle line:90%
Let me find the eraser here.

00:03:42.035 --> 00:03:50.520 align:middle line:90%


00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:51.020 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:55.840 align:middle line:90%


00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:59.370 align:middle line:84%
In one dimension,
we have time t.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.910 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to
think of a process

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:07.900 align:middle line:90%
Bt, which will start at 0.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:13.620 align:middle line:84%
And then it's going to evolve
over time in such a way

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:21.000 align:middle line:84%
that the increments
of the process from t

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.130 align:middle line:90%
to t plus delta t--

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:26.790 align:middle line:84%
so if we have t and
t plus delta t here,

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:33.230 align:middle line:84%
and we look at B of t here
and B of t plus delta t--

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.790 align:middle line:90%


00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:41.460 align:middle line:84%
this vertical increment is
going to be normally distributed

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:45.820 align:middle line:84%
for any increment
of time delta t.

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:51.450 align:middle line:84%
And the normal distribution for
the magnitude of that increment

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:55.480 align:middle line:84%
will be a normal random
variable with mean 0

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:59.950 align:middle line:84%
and a variance that is
proportional to the length

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:01.120 align:middle line:90%
of the interval.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:07.150 align:middle line:84%
So as we consider increments,
delta t that are larger

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:12.340 align:middle line:84%
will have greater variation in
the vertical increment that's

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:13.880 align:middle line:90%
normally distributed.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:17.600 align:middle line:84%
Now, in addition to
normal increments,

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.340 align:middle line:84%
we have independent,
disjoint increments.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:27.730 align:middle line:84%
So if we have different
time points, like t1,

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:33.310 align:middle line:90%
t2, and then t3, t4--

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:37.540 align:middle line:84%
if these increments are
separate or disjoint

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:42.640 align:middle line:84%
from each other, then
the change from t1 to t2

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:45.520 align:middle line:84%
and the change from
t3 to t4, those

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:49.600 align:middle line:84%
will be independent
random variables.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:57.370 align:middle line:84%
Finally, B of t turns out to be
a continuous function of time

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:00.050 align:middle line:84%
if it satisfies
these properties.

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:06.430 align:middle line:84%
And so this is a
very simple model

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:10.810 align:middle line:84%
in terms of a continuous
time stochastic process.

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:16.660 align:middle line:84%
Now, the properties
of Brownian motion

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:21.380 align:middle line:90%
are that it's a Markov process.

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:28.330 align:middle line:84%
So if we're looking at the
value at B of t plus delta

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:34.960 align:middle line:84%
t at this time point,
that is equal to the value

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.100 align:middle line:90%
at any prior time point--

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:38.830 align:middle line:90%
well, at B of t--

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:41.050 align:middle line:90%
plus the increment.

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:46.400 align:middle line:84%
And so given the information
set up to time little t,

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:50.500 align:middle line:84%
the conditional
distribution simply

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:55.450 align:middle line:84%
is equal to that
starting point, B of t,

00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:57.830 align:middle line:90%
plus the random increment.

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:03.050 align:middle line:84%
So displacements are
independent of the past,

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:05.583 align:middle line:84%
apart from the last
value of the past.

00:07:05.583 --> 00:07:09.010 align:middle line:90%


00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:09.700 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:16.930 align:middle line:84%
Mean and variance of B of t--
well, because the increments are

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:19.700 align:middle line:84%
normally distributed
with mean 0,

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:25.840 align:middle line:84%
then the expected value
of B of t into the future

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:27.890 align:middle line:90%
will stay at 0.

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:33.920 align:middle line:84%
But the variance of B of
t will increase with time,

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:39.650 align:middle line:84%
and so the standard deviation
of B of t will be proportional--

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:43.300 align:middle line:84%
or will be sigma times
the square root of t.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:49.040 align:middle line:84%
So if we think of
drawing curves which

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:52.590 align:middle line:84%
correspond to sigma
square root of t,

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:57.860 align:middle line:84%
this corresponds to 1
standard deviation above the 0

00:07:57.860 --> 00:07:59.143 align:middle line:90%
mean for the process.

00:07:59.143 --> 00:08:02.115 align:middle line:90%


00:08:02.115 --> 00:08:02.615 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:08:02.615 --> 00:08:06.300 align:middle line:84%
The covariance function
of Brownian motion--

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:11.330 align:middle line:84%
if we want to look at the
covariance between two times,

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:16.410 align:middle line:84%
s and t-- so suppose here is
time s, in here is time t--

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:27.530 align:middle line:84%
then the process paths
to time t include

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:31.010 align:middle line:84%
the process paths up to
time s, but then have

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:34.169 align:middle line:84%
this independent
increment beyond time s.

00:08:34.169 --> 00:08:38.000 align:middle line:84%
And so when we look
at the covariance

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:42.020 align:middle line:84%
of the process at
times s and t, it's

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:45.540 align:middle line:84%
equal to the expected
value of the product.

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:50.970 align:middle line:84%
But we can split B of t into
B of s plus the increment,

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:55.230 align:middle line:84%
and we simply get sigma
squared s for the covariance.

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:57.770 align:middle line:84%
So the degree to
which the process

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:02.760 align:middle line:84%
is correlated with other
values of the process,

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:06.710 align:middle line:84%
it depends on how much overlap
there is in the time intervals

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:10.130 align:middle line:84%
corresponding to
those two values.

00:09:10.130 --> 00:09:23.600 align:middle line:84%
Now, this process that starts at
0, B of 0 or B sub 0 or B of 0

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:28.880 align:middle line:84%
equal to 0, we could start
it at any other level

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:32.400 align:middle line:84%
and have a process that
has similar properties

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:36.480 align:middle line:84%
but just has a different
mean level from time 0.

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:41.810 align:middle line:84%
So we can talk about starting
the Brownian motion at any given

00:09:41.810 --> 00:09:44.240 align:middle line:90%
point.

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:48.950 align:middle line:84%
And so in order to
work with these models,

00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:53.870 align:middle line:84%
we'll find it convenient
to introduce the notation

00:09:53.870 --> 00:09:57.410 align:middle line:90%
for normal random variables.

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:03.530 align:middle line:84%
And so if we have Z be
a standard normal, that

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:08.070 align:middle line:84%
is a normal distribution with
mean 0 and standard deviation 1,

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:12.860 align:middle line:84%
then the probability
density function

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:15.840 align:middle line:90%
is given by this formula.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:19.020 align:middle line:84%
I'm sure everyone is
comfortable with that.

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:22.070 align:middle line:84%
And the cumulative
distribution function

00:10:22.070 --> 00:10:27.320 align:middle line:84%
is simply the integral of the
density from minus infinity

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:29.970 align:middle line:90%
up to c for phi of c.

00:10:29.970 --> 00:10:35.780 align:middle line:84%
And this notation of small
case phi and uppercase Phi are

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:39.920 align:middle line:84%
standard for referring to
Gaussian or normal densities

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:42.090 align:middle line:90%
in cdf's.

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:47.850 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we have a normal
with non-zero mean mu

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:52.710 align:middle line:84%
and variance tau squared,
or standard deviation tau,

00:10:52.710 --> 00:11:00.000 align:middle line:84%
then we have the
density of this normal Y

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:06.180 align:middle line:84%
is a simple function of the
density of the standard normal.

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:11.610 align:middle line:84%
We simply subtract the mean mu
and divide by tau inside phi,

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:16.300 align:middle line:84%
and we have a tau
denominator factor in front.

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:21.780 align:middle line:84%
And what's particularly
convenient at times

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:32.130 align:middle line:84%
is that, if we want to calculate
the cdf of Y, then this cdf of Y

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:37.950 align:middle line:84%
is actually the cdf under
the standard normal of c

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:41.130 align:middle line:90%
minus mu over tau.

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:54.220 align:middle line:84%
So we basically have Y is equal
to mu plus sigma or tau z.

00:11:54.220 --> 00:12:00.310 align:middle line:84%
And so z is equal to
Y minus mu over tau.

00:12:00.310 --> 00:12:06.300 align:middle line:84%
And so we're just substituting
in this equation for Y

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.550 align:middle line:90%
in terms of z.

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:14.190 align:middle line:84%
Now, the probability model
of the whole Brownian motion

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:19.450 align:middle line:90%
process is given by--

00:12:19.450 --> 00:12:23.220 align:middle line:84%
or we can represent it by saying
the conditional distribution

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:28.830 align:middle line:84%
of the time t value,
given the time s value,

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:34.830 align:middle line:84%
is normally distributed with
mean equal to the time s

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:38.460 align:middle line:84%
value, X, and variance
sigma squared times

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:41.730 align:middle line:90%
the length of the increment.

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:45.900 align:middle line:90%
So its pdf has this form.

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:53.370 align:middle line:84%
So the notation here is
the density of y at time t,

00:12:53.370 --> 00:12:58.020 align:middle line:90%
given x, the value at time s.

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:03.040 align:middle line:84%
We have this Gaussian
density that's centered at x.

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:10.170 align:middle line:84%
And the variance of y depends
on the increment from s

00:13:10.170 --> 00:13:15.330 align:middle line:84%
to t multiplied by
the sigma squared.

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:22.380 align:middle line:84%
Now, with this
probability model,

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:27.210 align:middle line:84%
it turns out this density
function satisfies the diffusion

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:28.920 align:middle line:90%
equation.

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:31.200 align:middle line:84%
And the diffusion
equation is going

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:37.600 align:middle line:84%
to arise again and again in
this course, especially when we

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:42.580 align:middle line:84%
get into the valuation
of derivatives.

00:13:42.580 --> 00:13:50.530 align:middle line:84%
And so for now, let's just
understand this diffusion

00:13:50.530 --> 00:13:51.620 align:middle line:90%
equation.

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:54.610 align:middle line:84%
It says that the
partial derivative

00:13:54.610 --> 00:14:02.650 align:middle line:84%
of p with respect to time
t is equal to a multiple

00:14:02.650 --> 00:14:08.500 align:middle line:84%
of the second partial derivative
of p with respect to y.

00:14:08.500 --> 00:14:15.460 align:middle line:84%
So this is easy to verify for
s equals 0 and sigma equals 1.

00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:22.150 align:middle line:84%
And if we look at
this set of graphs,

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:30.760 align:middle line:90%
here's the density of X, given--

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:35.398 align:middle line:84%
or let's say, here's,
essentially, the density of Y

00:14:35.398 --> 00:14:40.000 align:middle line:84%
represented by X. So
the notation symbol here

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:42.950 align:middle line:84%
is we're using X
now instead of Y.

00:14:42.950 --> 00:14:48.610 align:middle line:84%
And we're considering the
initial value of the Brownian

00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:49.845 align:middle line:90%
motion being 0.

00:14:49.845 --> 00:14:52.870 align:middle line:90%


00:14:52.870 --> 00:15:00.190 align:middle line:84%
And if we increase time,
then this normal density

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:07.870 align:middle line:84%
drops in the middle and it
increases far from the middle.

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:14.320 align:middle line:84%
And so the change
in the density is

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:22.460 align:middle line:84%
negative near the middle
and positive outside.

00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:28.390 align:middle line:84%
And that's because, if we
look at the second derivative

00:15:28.390 --> 00:15:33.070 align:middle line:84%
of this density
function, it's positive,

00:15:33.070 --> 00:15:36.050 align:middle line:84%
then it's negative for
the second derivative.

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:37.700 align:middle line:90%
The curvature is negative.

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:42.040 align:middle line:84%
And then it switches from
being a negative curvature

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:43.970 align:middle line:90%
to a positive curvature.

00:15:43.970 --> 00:15:48.370 align:middle line:84%
And those points of inflection
with the normal density

00:15:48.370 --> 00:15:52.960 align:middle line:84%
are actually at plus and
minus 1 standard deviation.

00:15:52.960 --> 00:16:03.070 align:middle line:84%
So if we increase the time
from 1 to 2 to 3, or 2.5 here,

00:16:03.070 --> 00:16:08.860 align:middle line:84%
you can see how it's
basically dropping

00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:10.420 align:middle line:84%
in the middle of
the distribution

00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:13.240 align:middle line:84%
and it's rising
on the other side.

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:17.650 align:middle line:84%
So this kind of
motion or dynamics

00:16:17.650 --> 00:16:21.400 align:middle line:84%
satisfies the
diffusion equation.

00:16:21.400 --> 00:16:26.800 align:middle line:84%
And so what's, I
think, important

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:31.430 align:middle line:84%
is just to have a
good sense of what

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:35.570 align:middle line:84%
it means when a function
such as the density

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:40.310 align:middle line:84%
satisfies this
diffusion equation.

00:16:40.310 --> 00:16:44.610 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see.

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:50.960 align:middle line:84%
This diffusion equation
relates to a conservation

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:54.110 align:middle line:90%
of probability for every x.

00:16:54.110 --> 00:17:00.050 align:middle line:84%
So regardless of what
the starting value is,

00:17:00.050 --> 00:17:04.849 align:middle line:84%
we have the probability density
integrating to 1 always.

00:17:04.849 --> 00:17:12.950 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that this
density is a unique solution

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:15.060 align:middle line:90%
to this diffusion equation.

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:22.490 align:middle line:84%
So an important issue in
the mathematical development

00:17:22.490 --> 00:17:27.660 align:middle line:84%
framework for models is our
solutions to our equations.

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:28.740 align:middle line:90%
Do they exist?

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:29.940 align:middle line:90%
Are they unique?

00:17:29.940 --> 00:17:32.480 align:middle line:84%
In fact, for this
diffusion equation,

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:38.930 align:middle line:84%
we get a unique solution
satisfying the properties.

00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:41.155 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:17:41.155 --> 00:17:43.760 align:middle line:84%
In other disciplines--
well, in physics,

00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:44.795 align:middle line:90%
there's a heat equation.

00:17:44.795 --> 00:17:47.660 align:middle line:90%


00:17:47.660 --> 00:17:48.710 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:17:48.710 --> 00:17:52.970 align:middle line:84%
In the dynamics of
populations, there's

00:17:52.970 --> 00:17:57.200 align:middle line:84%
the Fokker-Planck equation and
Kolmogorov Forward equation.

00:17:57.200 --> 00:18:03.350 align:middle line:84%
These arise outside of
mathematical Brownian motion

00:18:03.350 --> 00:18:04.290 align:middle line:90%
as well.

00:18:04.290 --> 00:18:10.730 align:middle line:84%
Now, the interesting
question perhaps,

00:18:10.730 --> 00:18:12.980 align:middle line:84%
or at least the interesting
question to mathematicians

00:18:12.980 --> 00:18:20.750 align:middle line:84%
is, how do we really define
Brownian motion formally

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:23.480 align:middle line:90%
as a stochastic process?

00:18:23.480 --> 00:18:27.620 align:middle line:84%
And so it turns out
that Brownian motion

00:18:27.620 --> 00:18:33.470 align:middle line:84%
can be defined as the
limiting stochastic process

00:18:33.470 --> 00:18:37.550 align:middle line:90%
corresponding to a random walk.

00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:43.490 align:middle line:84%
So let's consider
random variables

00:18:43.490 --> 00:18:47.420 align:middle line:84%
X indexed by integers
that are independent

00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:49.670 align:middle line:90%
and identically distributed.

00:18:49.670 --> 00:18:55.130 align:middle line:84%
And let's assume that
those X's have mean

00:18:55.130 --> 00:18:58.400 align:middle line:90%
0 and standard deviation 1.

00:18:58.400 --> 00:19:05.930 align:middle line:84%
Then, if we look at the
random walk with steps X1

00:19:05.930 --> 00:19:11.340 align:middle line:84%
to Xn for the n-th position
of the random walk,

00:19:11.340 --> 00:19:18.620 align:middle line:84%
then the limiting distribution
of the normalized random walk

00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:23.400 align:middle line:84%
is actually a standard
normal distribution.

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:29.100 align:middle line:84%
So phi of c is our standard
normal cumulative distribution

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:30.040 align:middle line:90%
function.

00:19:30.040 --> 00:19:35.220 align:middle line:84%
The standardized random
walk will in the limit,

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:40.650 align:middle line:84%
as n grows large, have
a Gaussian distribution

00:19:40.650 --> 00:19:44.130 align:middle line:84%
with mean 0 and
standard deviation 1.

00:19:44.130 --> 00:19:50.070 align:middle line:84%
So what we can do is
consider normalizing

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:59.460 align:middle line:84%
the random-walk
process by having

00:19:59.460 --> 00:20:06.510 align:middle line:84%
the partial sums of the
steps divided by root n.

00:20:06.510 --> 00:20:11.850 align:middle line:84%
But rather than focusing just on
those increments, those integer

00:20:11.850 --> 00:20:20.490 align:middle line:84%
increments, we consider four
times, say, between 0 and 1--

00:20:20.490 --> 00:20:28.720 align:middle line:84%
we can consider values
for time t between, say,

00:20:28.720 --> 00:20:33.610 align:middle line:84%
integer k over n and integer
k over n plus 1 over n

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:44.760 align:middle line:84%
to define a continuous path of
Bn of t, given s of n and t.

00:20:44.760 --> 00:20:48.100 align:middle line:84%
And so what we
then can do is say,

00:20:48.100 --> 00:20:54.120 align:middle line:84%
let's consider the limit as
the number of increments--

00:20:54.120 --> 00:20:57.790 align:middle line:84%
number of steps
increases arbitrarily.

00:20:57.790 --> 00:21:02.230 align:middle line:84%
Let's consider the
limiting process.

00:21:02.230 --> 00:21:07.050 align:middle line:84%
And that turns out to be
a Brownian motion process.

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:12.610 align:middle line:84%
So with this normalized
random-walk process,

00:21:12.610 --> 00:21:21.300 align:middle line:84%
it turns out that this
limiting distribution is--

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:25.620 align:middle line:84%
or this limiting process is
invariant to the distribution

00:21:25.620 --> 00:21:27.540 align:middle line:90%
of the X's.

00:21:27.540 --> 00:21:32.790 align:middle line:84%
So if we have random walks
with independent steps

00:21:32.790 --> 00:21:36.390 align:middle line:84%
that have mean 0,
standard deviation 1,

00:21:36.390 --> 00:21:41.050 align:middle line:84%
then regardless of what
that distribution is,

00:21:41.050 --> 00:21:44.040 align:middle line:84%
the limiting
normalized random walk

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:46.890 align:middle line:90%
will be the diffusion process.

00:21:46.890 --> 00:21:52.140 align:middle line:84%
So let's take a look at two
cases for the steps, Bernoulli

00:21:52.140 --> 00:21:59.490 align:middle line:84%
X's and Gaussian X's, which
of course will be Gaussian.

00:21:59.490 --> 00:22:11.880 align:middle line:84%
And so here is a cumulation or
a random walk with 10 steps.

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:18.430 align:middle line:84%
The top panel corresponds to
unit plus 1 or minus 1 steps,

00:22:18.430 --> 00:22:22.833 align:middle line:84%
and the bottom panel
corresponds to Gaussian steps.

00:22:22.833 --> 00:22:26.860 align:middle line:90%


00:22:26.860 --> 00:22:29.320 align:middle line:84%
And if we increase
the number of steps

00:22:29.320 --> 00:22:34.810 align:middle line:84%
and normalize them by
dividing by root t,

00:22:34.810 --> 00:22:39.860 align:middle line:84%
we end up getting these
different paths tracing out.

00:22:39.860 --> 00:22:42.820 align:middle line:84%
And so what's important
in this display is

00:22:42.820 --> 00:22:47.090 align:middle line:84%
that, as we let n get
arbitrarily large,

00:22:47.090 --> 00:22:51.020 align:middle line:84%
the process will be
equivalent to Brownian motion.

00:22:51.020 --> 00:22:56.830 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to go
up to 2,000 steps here.

00:22:56.830 --> 00:23:02.980 align:middle line:84%
And one can see how this
normalized random-walk process

00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:05.320 align:middle line:90%
evolves.

00:23:05.320 --> 00:23:12.310 align:middle line:84%
And when we get to the end of
the simulation here of 2,000

00:23:12.310 --> 00:23:16.180 align:middle line:90%
steps, this is what we have.

00:23:16.180 --> 00:23:23.920 align:middle line:84%
And so the key takeaway is
that the limiting process will

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:30.880 align:middle line:84%
be a Brownian motion, and it
doesn't matter what the step

00:23:30.880 --> 00:23:34.570 align:middle line:90%
distribution is in the limit.

00:23:34.570 --> 00:23:39.490 align:middle line:84%
And so this implies
that this model

00:23:39.490 --> 00:23:43.060 align:middle line:84%
may be useful in a
variety of contexts, where

00:23:43.060 --> 00:23:46.960 align:middle line:84%
underlying steps of
the process increments

00:23:46.960 --> 00:23:52.000 align:middle line:84%
may have some distribution
that's non-Gaussian.

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:56.470 align:middle line:84%
But the distribution
of increments

00:23:56.470 --> 00:24:01.586 align:middle line:84%
over long periods of time will
then follow the diffusion.

00:24:01.586 --> 00:24:06.700 align:middle line:90%


00:24:06.700 --> 00:24:09.160 align:middle line:84%
So the key properties
of Brownian motion

00:24:09.160 --> 00:24:10.670 align:middle line:90%
are independent increments.

00:24:10.670 --> 00:24:14.200 align:middle line:84%
And the variance
of those increments

00:24:14.200 --> 00:24:19.810 align:middle line:84%
are proportional to the time
period between the measures.

00:24:19.810 --> 00:24:25.300 align:middle line:84%
Now, there's a
reflection principle

00:24:25.300 --> 00:24:30.940 align:middle line:84%
in working with Brownian
motion that is very powerful.

00:24:30.940 --> 00:24:36.430 align:middle line:84%
This is an example where
the mathematical theory

00:24:36.430 --> 00:24:40.730 align:middle line:90%
is a bit tricky.

00:24:40.730 --> 00:24:45.490 align:middle line:84%
But if you're clever
at solving the problem,

00:24:45.490 --> 00:24:50.000 align:middle line:84%
you may find an elegant,
simple solution to the problem.

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:53.980 align:middle line:84%
So let's just understand what
it means to be reflecting

00:24:53.980 --> 00:24:56.620 align:middle line:90%
Brownian motion first.

00:24:56.620 --> 00:25:07.185 align:middle line:84%
So we consider Brownian motion
to be the process B of t.

00:25:07.185 --> 00:25:13.000 align:middle line:90%


00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:18.200 align:middle line:84%
And so this will be one
realization of the path perhaps.

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:28.370 align:middle line:84%
And then if we consider the
first time the process hits

00:25:28.370 --> 00:25:38.270 align:middle line:84%
x and define tau equal
to the first time B of t

00:25:38.270 --> 00:25:46.100 align:middle line:84%
is equal to x, then, OK,
this is the first time it

00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:49.070 align:middle line:90%
hits that level x.

00:25:49.070 --> 00:25:57.230 align:middle line:84%
If we consider reflecting this
path about the level at time

00:25:57.230 --> 00:25:59.340 align:middle line:90%
x going into the future--

00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:07.230 align:middle line:84%
so this would be going like
this and then up like that--

00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:13.130 align:middle line:84%
This is the mathematical formula
for doing a reflection of B

00:26:13.130 --> 00:26:15.290 align:middle line:90%
be about x.

00:26:15.290 --> 00:26:20.430 align:middle line:84%
And we'll see some examples
of this in a minute.

00:26:20.430 --> 00:26:25.850 align:middle line:84%
But what is important
in these reflected

00:26:25.850 --> 00:26:30.440 align:middle line:84%
paths is that the
probability model

00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:35.580 align:middle line:84%
for the original process
and the reflected process,

00:26:35.580 --> 00:26:38.480 align:middle line:90%
those are identical.

00:26:38.480 --> 00:26:44.180 align:middle line:84%
So let's take a look at this
reflected Brownian motion

00:26:44.180 --> 00:26:45.000 align:middle line:90%
simulation.

00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:50.330 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm doing here is
simulating Brownian motion over

00:26:50.330 --> 00:26:57.810 align:middle line:84%
10,000 steps and looking at
an original process in red,

00:26:57.810 --> 00:27:00.900 align:middle line:84%
and then reflecting
that in green.

00:27:00.900 --> 00:27:05.330 align:middle line:84%
And because of overlapping
colors, I guess,

00:27:05.330 --> 00:27:10.430 align:middle line:84%
the beginning process at
time 0 is the original

00:27:10.430 --> 00:27:11.820 align:middle line:90%
and the reflected path.

00:27:11.820 --> 00:27:14.100 align:middle line:90%
So it appears as the reflected.

00:27:14.100 --> 00:27:18.900 align:middle line:84%
So once it hits
this level x in red,

00:27:18.900 --> 00:27:23.940 align:middle line:84%
the green reflects
about x, the value.

00:27:23.940 --> 00:27:30.050 align:middle line:84%
And it continues reflecting
across the remaining

00:27:30.050 --> 00:27:34.190 align:middle line:90%
path of the original series.

00:27:34.190 --> 00:27:39.230 align:middle line:84%
Now, here's another realization
of reflected Brownian motion,

00:27:39.230 --> 00:27:42.260 align:middle line:90%
has the same property.

00:27:42.260 --> 00:27:46.560 align:middle line:90%
And here's a third.

00:27:46.560 --> 00:27:50.820 align:middle line:84%
Basically, with different
realizations of Brownian motion,

00:27:50.820 --> 00:27:53.690 align:middle line:90%
we'll get different paths.

00:27:53.690 --> 00:27:57.590 align:middle line:84%
And the reflected paths
have these same properties.

00:27:57.590 --> 00:28:05.525 align:middle line:84%
Now, the interesting
question is--

00:28:05.525 --> 00:28:08.250 align:middle line:90%


00:28:08.250 --> 00:28:12.270 align:middle line:84%
well, it's actually-- let me
go back to the notes here.

00:28:12.270 --> 00:28:17.640 align:middle line:84%
And the interesting
question is to ask,

00:28:17.640 --> 00:28:28.800 align:middle line:84%
what is the maximum of a
Brownian motion up to time t?

00:28:28.800 --> 00:28:32.100 align:middle line:84%
So we can define
m of t to be, what

00:28:32.100 --> 00:28:35.790 align:middle line:84%
is the maximum value that
the Brownian motion has

00:28:35.790 --> 00:28:40.380 align:middle line:90%
realized between time 0 and t?

00:28:40.380 --> 00:28:50.910 align:middle line:84%
Well, the probability of the
maximum exceeding x, some level,

00:28:50.910 --> 00:28:55.020 align:middle line:84%
will actually be
twice the probability

00:28:55.020 --> 00:28:59.860 align:middle line:84%
that the ending value of
the process exceeds x.

00:28:59.860 --> 00:29:04.863 align:middle line:84%
So let's take a look again
at the reflected paths.

00:29:04.863 --> 00:29:09.180 align:middle line:90%


00:29:09.180 --> 00:29:20.970 align:middle line:84%
So if we look at the reflected
paths about x, then the red

00:29:20.970 --> 00:29:27.376 align:middle line:84%
and the green paths have the
same probabilities of occurring.

00:29:27.376 --> 00:29:33.460 align:middle line:90%


00:29:33.460 --> 00:29:35.076 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:29:35.076 --> 00:29:41.220 align:middle line:84%
If we look at whether the ending
value of the original Brownian

00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:42.660 align:middle line:90%
motion--

00:29:42.660 --> 00:29:49.605 align:middle line:84%
sorry-- or the ending
value of a Brownian motion

00:29:49.605 --> 00:29:55.950 align:middle line:84%
exceeds a given
level, then there

00:29:55.950 --> 00:30:01.770 align:middle line:84%
will be twice that number,
twice those realizations

00:30:01.770 --> 00:30:05.080 align:middle line:84%
for which the maximum
exceeded that level.

00:30:05.080 --> 00:30:10.410 align:middle line:84%
So for both of these
reflected paths, the maximum--

00:30:10.410 --> 00:30:15.900 align:middle line:84%
let's see-- the maximum
of the path exceeded x.

00:30:15.900 --> 00:30:27.330 align:middle line:84%
And so looking here, the
probability of the maximum

00:30:27.330 --> 00:30:30.750 align:middle line:84%
exceeding x, well,
we can just look

00:30:30.750 --> 00:30:38.830 align:middle line:84%
at the probability of a path
exceeding x at the end point.

00:30:38.830 --> 00:30:44.020 align:middle line:84%
And the reflected intervals
will match those, one for one.

00:30:44.020 --> 00:30:47.340 align:middle line:84%
And so we basically
have the formula

00:30:47.340 --> 00:30:57.960 align:middle line:84%
that we have twice the final
value of the process exceeding

00:30:57.960 --> 00:31:03.070 align:middle line:84%
x is equal to the probability
that the maximum exceeds x.

00:31:03.070 --> 00:31:08.080 align:middle line:84%
And we can write out what
that probability is in terms

00:31:08.080 --> 00:31:10.403 align:middle line:90%
of the standard normal cdf.

00:31:10.403 --> 00:31:14.800 align:middle line:90%


00:31:14.800 --> 00:31:24.490 align:middle line:84%
Now, from this result, we can
differentiate this probability

00:31:24.490 --> 00:31:35.650 align:middle line:84%
with respect to x and get
the density of the maximum.

00:31:35.650 --> 00:31:40.780 align:middle line:90%
And so let's see here.

00:31:40.780 --> 00:31:47.080 align:middle line:84%
So here is a graph of the
cumulative probability

00:31:47.080 --> 00:31:53.030 align:middle line:84%
of the maximum
exceeding a level x.

00:31:53.030 --> 00:31:58.600 align:middle line:84%
And this is, basically,
we're interested in,

00:31:58.600 --> 00:32:02.450 align:middle line:84%
what's the probability of
exceeding x before time t?

00:32:02.450 --> 00:32:07.310 align:middle line:84%
And with lower
values of x, like 1,

00:32:07.310 --> 00:32:08.955 align:middle line:90%
the probability is quite high.

00:32:08.955 --> 00:32:12.610 align:middle line:90%


00:32:12.610 --> 00:32:13.250 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:32:13.250 --> 00:32:16.510 align:middle line:90%
If x is very--

00:32:16.510 --> 00:32:17.140 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:32:17.140 --> 00:32:18.220 align:middle line:90%
Or, sorry.

00:32:18.220 --> 00:32:19.605 align:middle line:90%
Is that x there?

00:32:19.605 --> 00:32:23.095 align:middle line:90%


00:32:23.095 --> 00:32:23.595 align:middle line:90%
Right.

00:32:23.595 --> 00:32:24.310 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:32:24.310 --> 00:32:29.620 align:middle line:84%
So if the maximum is 3, the
probability of exceeding 3

00:32:29.620 --> 00:32:30.700 align:middle line:90%
is much lower.

00:32:30.700 --> 00:32:37.360 align:middle line:84%
The probability of exceeding
2 is in the mid-way there.

00:32:37.360 --> 00:32:40.870 align:middle line:84%
And what's also
very interesting is

00:32:40.870 --> 00:32:47.710 align:middle line:84%
that we can use these results
to calculate the density

00:32:47.710 --> 00:32:54.880 align:middle line:84%
function for the first
hitting time of a given level.

00:32:54.880 --> 00:32:59.380 align:middle line:84%
And so importantly, we
have the probability

00:32:59.380 --> 00:33:01.630 align:middle line:84%
of the max up to
time t exceeding

00:33:01.630 --> 00:33:09.850 align:middle line:84%
x is twice the probability that
the ending value exceeds x.

00:33:09.850 --> 00:33:16.720 align:middle line:84%
And we can differentiate
this with respect to t.

00:33:16.720 --> 00:33:22.840 align:middle line:84%
And we get this density function
for the density of the stopping

00:33:22.840 --> 00:33:28.210 align:middle line:84%
time for hitting
a given level x.

00:33:28.210 --> 00:33:32.380 align:middle line:84%
So let's see here
if we have that.

00:33:32.380 --> 00:33:34.896 align:middle line:84%
So here's the hitting
time densities.

00:33:34.896 --> 00:33:37.540 align:middle line:90%


00:33:37.540 --> 00:33:39.610 align:middle line:84%
So with standard
Brownian motion,

00:33:39.610 --> 00:33:44.720 align:middle line:84%
if we're looking at the time to
hit the level 1 starting at 0,

00:33:44.720 --> 00:33:48.370 align:middle line:84%
then there's this nice
unimodal distribution

00:33:48.370 --> 00:33:51.800 align:middle line:84%
for that time, which
is relatively short.

00:33:51.800 --> 00:33:56.570 align:middle line:84%
But if we're looking at hitting
times for higher levels,

00:33:56.570 --> 00:34:02.030 align:middle line:84%
like two or three, then that
hitting time distribution

00:34:02.030 --> 00:34:03.540 align:middle line:90%
spreads out.

00:34:03.540 --> 00:34:05.910 align:middle line:90%
So now, let's see.

00:34:05.910 --> 00:34:12.620 align:middle line:84%
We can talk about some
extensions of Brownian motion.

00:34:12.620 --> 00:34:18.179 align:middle line:84%
One is just reflected
Brownian motion around 0.

00:34:18.179 --> 00:34:27.770 align:middle line:84%
So if we have the process
bouncing off 0 level,

00:34:27.770 --> 00:34:31.909 align:middle line:84%
we can think of
that process being

00:34:31.909 --> 00:34:35.159 align:middle line:90%
a positive stochastic process.

00:34:35.159 --> 00:34:40.670 align:middle line:84%
And it's fairly easy to show
that the expected value of this

00:34:40.670 --> 00:34:45.530 align:middle line:84%
reflected Brownian motion
is a multiple of root

00:34:45.530 --> 00:34:50.239 align:middle line:84%
t, square root of 2 over pi,
and the variance is 1 minus 2

00:34:50.239 --> 00:34:53.050 align:middle line:90%
over pi t.

00:34:53.050 --> 00:34:59.390 align:middle line:84%
And we can also consider
absorbed Brownian motion.

00:34:59.390 --> 00:35:05.330 align:middle line:84%
If we consider tau being
the first time the level 0--

00:35:05.330 --> 00:35:15.170 align:middle line:84%
or level x is hit, then we
can define our process being

00:35:15.170 --> 00:35:21.920 align:middle line:84%
equal to the original Brownian
motion B of t, or B at 0 for t

00:35:21.920 --> 00:35:23.450 align:middle line:90%
greater than tau.

00:35:23.450 --> 00:35:30.170 align:middle line:84%
And so with absorbed Brownian
motion, these kinds of processes

00:35:30.170 --> 00:35:34.550 align:middle line:84%
are useful, for
example, when we have

00:35:34.550 --> 00:35:39.530 align:middle line:84%
a Brownian motion-like process
for a stock price, which

00:35:39.530 --> 00:35:42.960 align:middle line:84%
is generally positive, so
long as the company is viable.

00:35:42.960 --> 00:35:44.550 align:middle line:84%
But if the company
goes bankrupt,

00:35:44.550 --> 00:35:48.800 align:middle line:84%
then the price
gets absorbed at 0.

00:35:48.800 --> 00:35:57.470 align:middle line:84%
Now, we have another extension
of Brownian motion, which

00:35:57.470 --> 00:36:01.340 align:middle line:90%
is called the Brownian Bridge.

00:36:01.340 --> 00:36:12.680 align:middle line:84%
And so if we consider a Brownian
motion process starting at 0,

00:36:12.680 --> 00:36:23.240 align:middle line:84%
and it goes up to, let's
say, time 1, starting at 0,

00:36:23.240 --> 00:36:24.313 align:middle line:90%
we can define--

00:36:24.313 --> 00:36:27.680 align:middle line:90%


00:36:27.680 --> 00:36:38.040 align:middle line:84%
we can define the procedure
x, which is equal to B of t--

00:36:38.040 --> 00:36:41.930 align:middle line:90%


00:36:41.930 --> 00:36:50.270 align:middle line:84%
let's see-- B of
t minus tB of 1.

00:36:50.270 --> 00:36:50.790 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:36:50.790 --> 00:36:52.384 align:middle line:90%
Is that right?

00:36:52.384 --> 00:36:55.440 align:middle line:90%
Minus t times B of 1.

00:36:55.440 --> 00:36:58.474 align:middle line:90%
So if we consider--

00:36:58.474 --> 00:37:05.400 align:middle line:90%


00:37:05.400 --> 00:37:07.290 align:middle line:90%
let's see-- looking at--

00:37:07.290 --> 00:37:08.070 align:middle line:90%
well, let's see.

00:37:08.070 --> 00:37:13.710 align:middle line:84%
This process, so x
of 1 is equal to B

00:37:13.710 --> 00:37:20.400 align:middle line:84%
of 1 minus 1 times B of
1, which is equal to 0.

00:37:20.400 --> 00:37:26.553 align:middle line:90%
If we basically draw a line to--

00:37:26.553 --> 00:37:29.730 align:middle line:90%


00:37:29.730 --> 00:37:38.220 align:middle line:84%
if we consider this line as
1 and this has slope B of 1,

00:37:38.220 --> 00:37:43.680 align:middle line:84%
then if we subtract
tB of 1, we're

00:37:43.680 --> 00:37:50.400 align:middle line:84%
looking at pinning down
the Brownian motion

00:37:50.400 --> 00:37:59.070 align:middle line:84%
and looking at the distance
from a constant slope, tB of 1

00:37:59.070 --> 00:38:00.700 align:middle line:90%
and B of t.

00:38:00.700 --> 00:38:05.280 align:middle line:84%
And this particular
process actually

00:38:05.280 --> 00:38:11.400 align:middle line:84%
arises in a very
interesting context

00:38:11.400 --> 00:38:15.310 align:middle line:84%
of normalized empirical
distribution functions.

00:38:15.310 --> 00:38:23.170 align:middle line:84%
So what do we want
to highlight here?

00:38:23.170 --> 00:38:28.620 align:middle line:84%
If we have a sequence
of random variables

00:38:28.620 --> 00:38:36.870 align:middle line:84%
that are uniformly
distributed between 0 and 1

00:38:36.870 --> 00:38:43.230 align:middle line:84%
and we calculate their empirical
distribution function--

00:38:43.230 --> 00:38:49.410 align:middle line:84%
so it's the count of the
U's less than or equal to

00:38:49.410 --> 00:38:51.960 align:middle line:90%
t divided by n--

00:38:51.960 --> 00:38:58.080 align:middle line:84%
then this empirical
distribution function

00:38:58.080 --> 00:39:08.400 align:middle line:84%
will have expectation equal to t
and variance equal to t times 1

00:39:08.400 --> 00:39:10.120 align:middle line:90%
minus t over n.

00:39:10.120 --> 00:39:17.890 align:middle line:90%
So if we think of these U's--

00:39:17.890 --> 00:39:24.180 align:middle line:84%
so we have Ui is distributed
uniform from 0 to 1.

00:39:24.180 --> 00:39:27.690 align:middle line:90%
We have the scale from 0 to 1.

00:39:27.690 --> 00:39:34.110 align:middle line:84%
And we consider a value
Ui as a realization.

00:39:34.110 --> 00:39:43.414 align:middle line:84%
Then the probability that Ui
is less than or equal to t

00:39:43.414 --> 00:39:45.050 align:middle line:90%
is equal to t.

00:39:45.050 --> 00:39:51.190 align:middle line:90%


00:39:51.190 --> 00:40:01.480 align:middle line:84%
And the variance
of the indicator

00:40:01.480 --> 00:40:04.460 align:middle line:84%
of Ui being less
than or equal to t,

00:40:04.460 --> 00:40:09.460 align:middle line:84%
this is a Bernoulli
outcome, which

00:40:09.460 --> 00:40:14.650 align:middle line:84%
has variance equal to the
probability of occurring

00:40:14.650 --> 00:40:17.840 align:middle line:90%
times the complement of that.

00:40:17.840 --> 00:40:26.770 align:middle line:84%
So we get this t times 1 minus
t being the variance of--

00:40:26.770 --> 00:40:30.970 align:middle line:84%
the indicator of the Ui being
less than or equal to t.

00:40:30.970 --> 00:40:38.930 align:middle line:84%
And then if we consider the
empirical distribution function,

00:40:38.930 --> 00:40:42.530 align:middle line:84%
which is summing over
these indicators,

00:40:42.530 --> 00:40:54.130 align:middle line:84%
then we get a process which
has a limiting value, which

00:40:54.130 --> 00:41:01.360 align:middle line:84%
is basically the deviation from
its mean, scaled up by root n,

00:41:01.360 --> 00:41:05.710 align:middle line:84%
is normal with mean 0 and
variance t times 1 minus t.

00:41:05.710 --> 00:41:10.780 align:middle line:84%
And this is actually the
Brownian Bridge process.

00:41:10.780 --> 00:41:18.490 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see.

00:41:18.490 --> 00:41:25.210 align:middle line:84%
With these examples where we
use uniform random variables

00:41:25.210 --> 00:41:30.550 align:middle line:84%
and properties of functions
of those uniforms,

00:41:30.550 --> 00:41:39.400 align:middle line:84%
it's important to note that for
any continuous distribution,

00:41:39.400 --> 00:41:45.985 align:middle line:90%
say, Y distributed with some--

00:41:45.985 --> 00:41:48.740 align:middle line:90%


00:41:48.740 --> 00:42:02.930 align:middle line:84%
this is the cdf of Y.
If we calculate F of Y,

00:42:02.930 --> 00:42:06.276 align:middle line:84%
this happens to be a
uniform distribution.

00:42:06.276 --> 00:42:09.260 align:middle line:90%


00:42:09.260 --> 00:42:12.680 align:middle line:84%
So this is the probability
integral transform

00:42:12.680 --> 00:42:14.580 align:middle line:90%
of any random variable.

00:42:14.580 --> 00:42:19.100 align:middle line:84%
And so when we
have this property

00:42:19.100 --> 00:42:24.680 align:middle line:84%
for the empirical distribution
function of uniforms--

00:42:24.680 --> 00:42:31.010 align:middle line:84%
well, of uniforms, if
we replace the U's,

00:42:31.010 --> 00:42:35.210 align:middle line:84%
which are uniform
0, 1 by assumption,

00:42:35.210 --> 00:42:39.830 align:middle line:84%
with a distribution
function F of Y, where

00:42:39.830 --> 00:42:49.160 align:middle line:84%
we have Y1 to Yn iid,
with this cdf F sub Y,

00:42:49.160 --> 00:43:00.780 align:middle line:84%
then the Ui equal to
F of Yi F sub Y of i,

00:43:00.780 --> 00:43:02.283 align:middle line:90%
these will be iid uniform.

00:43:02.283 --> 00:43:05.210 align:middle line:90%


00:43:05.210 --> 00:43:11.600 align:middle line:84%
So that's a really
nice result. And it

00:43:11.600 --> 00:43:19.130 align:middle line:84%
leads to issues involved with
modeling model selection.

00:43:19.130 --> 00:43:23.060 align:middle line:84%
If we want to know whether a
given an assumed probability

00:43:23.060 --> 00:43:27.590 align:middle line:84%
model for Y's is
true or not, we can

00:43:27.590 --> 00:43:33.230 align:middle line:84%
do this transformation and test
whether the transformed series

00:43:33.230 --> 00:43:37.610 align:middle line:84%
or transformed sample is
consistent with being uniform

00:43:37.610 --> 00:43:38.125 align:middle line:90%
or not.

00:43:38.125 --> 00:43:40.730 align:middle line:90%


00:43:40.730 --> 00:43:42.650 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:43:42.650 --> 00:43:48.140 align:middle line:84%
Well, with simple Brownian
motion, standard Brownian

00:43:48.140 --> 00:43:51.290 align:middle line:84%
motion, we can
consider an extension

00:43:51.290 --> 00:43:56.940 align:middle line:84%
of that to Brownian
motion with drift, mu.

00:43:56.940 --> 00:44:00.950 align:middle line:84%
So we can consider a drift
parameter and a volatility

00:44:00.950 --> 00:44:03.740 align:middle line:90%
parameter.

00:44:03.740 --> 00:44:12.500 align:middle line:84%
And such a process will
have independent increments,

00:44:12.500 --> 00:44:20.240 align:middle line:84%
with variances of differences
proportional to the time

00:44:20.240 --> 00:44:21.770 align:middle line:90%
interval between s and t.

00:44:21.770 --> 00:44:32.570 align:middle line:84%
And we can work with
the Brownian motion

00:44:32.570 --> 00:44:40.770 align:middle line:84%
with drift using a
one-step analysis.

00:44:40.770 --> 00:44:48.510 align:middle line:84%
And in considering
this X process--

00:44:48.510 --> 00:44:56.790 align:middle line:84%
so X is equal to t
mu plus sigma B of t.

00:44:56.790 --> 00:44:58.970 align:middle line:90%
So this is X of t.

00:44:58.970 --> 00:45:02.460 align:middle line:90%


00:45:02.460 --> 00:45:09.510 align:middle line:84%
If we look at the increment
ahead delta t from little t,

00:45:09.510 --> 00:45:15.600 align:middle line:84%
then expanding out the
representation of this Brownian

00:45:15.600 --> 00:45:20.070 align:middle line:84%
motion with drift,
we get that it's

00:45:20.070 --> 00:45:27.330 align:middle line:84%
equal to the Brownian motion at
time little t plus mu delta t

00:45:27.330 --> 00:45:33.780 align:middle line:84%
plus sigma times the increment
of the Brownian motion process

00:45:33.780 --> 00:45:37.620 align:middle line:90%
from t to t plus delta t.

00:45:37.620 --> 00:45:43.710 align:middle line:84%
And so increments
of X correspond

00:45:43.710 --> 00:45:50.820 align:middle line:84%
to increments of time plus
times the drift rate, mu,

00:45:50.820 --> 00:45:53.550 align:middle line:84%
plus sigma, the
volatility parameter,

00:45:53.550 --> 00:45:58.830 align:middle line:84%
times the increment of the
standard Brownian motion.

00:45:58.830 --> 00:46:04.890 align:middle line:84%
So our process X,
Brownian with drift,

00:46:04.890 --> 00:46:10.350 align:middle line:84%
will have non-zero
mean increments

00:46:10.350 --> 00:46:13.530 align:middle line:84%
and increments
variances that are

00:46:13.530 --> 00:46:17.080 align:middle line:84%
proportional to
the change in time.

00:46:17.080 --> 00:46:24.180 align:middle line:84%
And this increment will have
an exact normal distribution

00:46:24.180 --> 00:46:26.160 align:middle line:90%
with these properties.

00:46:26.160 --> 00:46:33.300 align:middle line:84%
And what's particularly
important is that,

00:46:33.300 --> 00:46:39.270 align:middle line:84%
as we let delta t, the
length of our increments,

00:46:39.270 --> 00:46:48.330 align:middle line:84%
get infinitesimal, then the
expected squared increment of X

00:46:48.330 --> 00:46:50.770 align:middle line:90%
ends up being equal to--

00:46:50.770 --> 00:46:55.380 align:middle line:84%
well, it's exactly equal to
the variance of that increment

00:46:55.380 --> 00:46:57.900 align:middle line:90%
plus the square of the mean.

00:46:57.900 --> 00:47:03.870 align:middle line:84%
But the square of
the mean is a term

00:47:03.870 --> 00:47:08.040 align:middle line:90%
that is small order of delta t.

00:47:08.040 --> 00:47:12.570 align:middle line:84%
So our expected
squared increments

00:47:12.570 --> 00:47:18.780 align:middle line:84%
will be to order
delta t equal to sigma

00:47:18.780 --> 00:47:24.780 align:middle line:84%
squared delta t and then a
small order of delta t term

00:47:24.780 --> 00:47:25.870 align:middle line:90%
after that.

00:47:25.870 --> 00:47:30.990 align:middle line:84%
Also, if we consider
powers of delta X,

00:47:30.990 --> 00:47:36.640 align:middle line:84%
so our increments to
a power, then when

00:47:36.640 --> 00:47:40.040 align:middle line:90%
our power c is greater than 2--

00:47:40.040 --> 00:47:42.710 align:middle line:84%
it's equal to 2 here--
when it's greater than 2,

00:47:42.710 --> 00:47:47.480 align:middle line:84%
then this will also be
a small order delta t.

00:47:47.480 --> 00:47:53.920 align:middle line:84%
Now, the reason why we're
interested in these properties

00:47:53.920 --> 00:48:02.770 align:middle line:84%
is because we're going to be
working, ultimately, with, say,

00:48:02.770 --> 00:48:13.360 align:middle line:84%
a value of some derivative
that depends upon X of t.

00:48:13.360 --> 00:48:22.150 align:middle line:84%
So there's basically a
derivative value, which

00:48:22.150 --> 00:48:33.400 align:middle line:90%
depends upon the underlying--

00:48:33.400 --> 00:48:37.960 align:middle line:84%
I'll just say the
underlying asset price.

00:48:37.960 --> 00:48:45.700 align:middle line:84%
And so the dynamics of
the derivative prices

00:48:45.700 --> 00:48:51.630 align:middle line:84%
can be written as
a Taylor series.

00:48:51.630 --> 00:48:55.900 align:middle line:90%


00:48:55.900 --> 00:49:00.970 align:middle line:84%
And so we'll want to be able
to deal with increments of X

00:49:00.970 --> 00:49:10.990 align:middle line:84%
and powers of increments of X
as our valuation function may

00:49:10.990 --> 00:49:15.150 align:middle line:84%
be expressed, if it has a
Taylor series representation.

00:49:15.150 --> 00:49:20.170 align:middle line:90%


00:49:20.170 --> 00:49:21.165 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:49:21.165 --> 00:49:29.050 align:middle line:84%
So let's consider the problem
of Brownian motion underlying

00:49:29.050 --> 00:49:31.600 align:middle line:90%
a gambler's ruin problem.

00:49:31.600 --> 00:49:38.350 align:middle line:84%
So let's let X of t be
Brownian motion with drift, mu

00:49:38.350 --> 00:49:41.350 align:middle line:90%
and variance sigma squared.

00:49:41.350 --> 00:49:47.740 align:middle line:84%
And we'll consider
an initial level

00:49:47.740 --> 00:49:51.345 align:middle line:84%
equal to X that's between
two levels, a and b.

00:49:51.345 --> 00:49:58.750 align:middle line:90%


00:49:58.750 --> 00:50:09.520 align:middle line:84%
And we want to consider the
stopping time hitting a or b

00:50:09.520 --> 00:50:16.660 align:middle line:84%
and ask, what's the probability
that we hit b first?

00:50:16.660 --> 00:50:27.640 align:middle line:84%
So over time t, we have,
basically, levels a

00:50:27.640 --> 00:50:35.000 align:middle line:84%
and b and an initial
level X at time 0.

00:50:35.000 --> 00:50:42.560 align:middle line:84%
And we're interested
in hitting a or b

00:50:42.560 --> 00:50:51.080 align:middle line:84%
so tau is equal to the
hit time of a or b.

00:50:51.080 --> 00:50:55.160 align:middle line:84%
And we're interested in,
what's the probability

00:50:55.160 --> 00:51:02.270 align:middle line:90%
of hitting b first before a?

00:51:02.270 --> 00:51:11.510 align:middle line:84%
And this generalizes our
problem in the first lecture

00:51:11.510 --> 00:51:16.880 align:middle line:84%
on stochastic processes, where
we had a random walk with unit

00:51:16.880 --> 00:51:18.540 align:middle line:90%
steps with drift.

00:51:18.540 --> 00:51:21.770 align:middle line:90%


00:51:21.770 --> 00:51:26.030 align:middle line:84%
So there was a bias of maybe
a plus 1 versus a minus 1

00:51:26.030 --> 00:51:26.880 align:middle line:90%
occurring.

00:51:26.880 --> 00:51:28.850 align:middle line:84%
And we wanted to
solve for, what's

00:51:28.850 --> 00:51:34.760 align:middle line:84%
the likelihood of hitting
a level b before level a?

00:51:34.760 --> 00:51:42.600 align:middle line:84%
So with this, we use an
infinitesimal one-step analysis.

00:51:42.600 --> 00:51:48.620 align:middle line:84%
And this solution approach
is illustrative of how many

00:51:48.620 --> 00:51:55.370 align:middle line:84%
problems are solved with
stochastic processes.

00:51:55.370 --> 00:52:03.710 align:middle line:84%
We can define u of x to be the
probability function, which

00:52:03.710 --> 00:52:11.780 align:middle line:84%
is the probability that the
stopped process equals b, given

00:52:11.780 --> 00:52:24.230 align:middle line:84%
that it starts at X. And
so if we consider u of x

00:52:24.230 --> 00:52:30.050 align:middle line:84%
and write it as the expected
value of u of x plus delta X,

00:52:30.050 --> 00:52:39.360 align:middle line:84%
then if our delta t time
increment is very, very short,

00:52:39.360 --> 00:52:46.730 align:middle line:84%
then this expectation
will equal u of x.

00:52:46.730 --> 00:52:52.010 align:middle line:84%
So the likelihood
of hitting b first

00:52:52.010 --> 00:52:59.420 align:middle line:84%
will be the expectation of u
of x, a very short increment

00:52:59.420 --> 00:53:01.140 align:middle line:90%
into the future.

00:53:01.140 --> 00:53:04.790 align:middle line:84%
And so if we take the
expectation with respect

00:53:04.790 --> 00:53:11.660 align:middle line:84%
to the delta X variable,
well, delta X is X of t

00:53:11.660 --> 00:53:15.020 align:middle line:90%
plus delta t minus X of t--

00:53:15.020 --> 00:53:24.060 align:middle line:84%
then if we assume that our
function u, this probability,

00:53:24.060 --> 00:53:29.520 align:middle line:84%
is twice differentiable,
then the Taylor

00:53:29.520 --> 00:53:36.240 align:middle line:84%
series about the center X
will equal u of x plus delta X

00:53:36.240 --> 00:53:41.040 align:middle line:84%
times the first derivative
plus a half delta

00:53:41.040 --> 00:53:43.270 align:middle line:84%
X squared times the
second derivative,

00:53:43.270 --> 00:53:50.130 align:middle line:84%
plus small order terms
of delta X squared.

00:53:50.130 --> 00:53:57.480 align:middle line:84%
And so if we take an
expectation of that first Taylor

00:53:57.480 --> 00:54:04.030 align:middle line:84%
series on both sides, well,
the u of x is a constant.

00:54:04.030 --> 00:54:07.740 align:middle line:84%
And then we have u prime
of x, a constant times

00:54:07.740 --> 00:54:10.890 align:middle line:84%
the expected value
of delta X. And then

00:54:10.890 --> 00:54:13.200 align:middle line:84%
plus a half the
expected value of delta

00:54:13.200 --> 00:54:16.830 align:middle line:90%
x squared times u double prime.

00:54:16.830 --> 00:54:21.480 align:middle line:84%
And we have mu delta t is
the expectation of delta

00:54:21.480 --> 00:54:26.940 align:middle line:84%
X. Sigma squared delta t
plus little o of delta t

00:54:26.940 --> 00:54:31.230 align:middle line:84%
is the expectation
of delta X squared.

00:54:31.230 --> 00:54:38.700 align:middle line:84%
So we have this expansion
from the Taylor series.

00:54:38.700 --> 00:54:48.510 align:middle line:84%
And from what we said before,
this expected value of u

00:54:48.510 --> 00:54:53.610 align:middle line:84%
evaluated at x plus delta X
should simply equal u of x.

00:54:53.610 --> 00:55:02.850 align:middle line:84%
And if that's true, then we
get this equation for u prime

00:55:02.850 --> 00:55:06.090 align:middle line:90%
and u double prime.

00:55:06.090 --> 00:55:16.140 align:middle line:84%
And if we take that equation
and u prime and u double prime

00:55:16.140 --> 00:55:19.260 align:middle line:84%
and we divide by
delta t, we then

00:55:19.260 --> 00:55:30.360 align:middle line:84%
get 0 is equal to mu u prime
of x plus a half sigma squared

00:55:30.360 --> 00:55:32.060 align:middle line:90%
u double prime of x.

00:55:32.060 --> 00:55:35.100 align:middle line:90%


00:55:35.100 --> 00:55:44.200 align:middle line:84%
And to be really precise, little
o factor to make it an equation.

00:55:44.200 --> 00:55:51.870 align:middle line:84%
Now, this equation here is
a second-order differential

00:55:51.870 --> 00:55:53.520 align:middle line:90%
equation.

00:55:53.520 --> 00:56:02.773 align:middle line:84%
And so what are solutions to
this second-order differential

00:56:02.773 --> 00:56:03.273 align:middle line:90%
equation?

00:56:03.273 --> 00:56:10.440 align:middle line:90%


00:56:10.440 --> 00:56:16.523 align:middle line:84%
This is an
interesting challenge.

00:56:16.523 --> 00:56:18.065 align:middle line:84%
Let me just get some
more chalk here.

00:56:18.065 --> 00:56:22.120 align:middle line:90%


00:56:22.120 --> 00:56:23.620 align:middle line:84%
It's maybe been
a number of years

00:56:23.620 --> 00:56:26.630 align:middle line:84%
since people have taken
differential equations, right?

00:56:26.630 --> 00:56:29.350 align:middle line:90%


00:56:29.350 --> 00:56:39.730 align:middle line:84%
But let's consider, let's see,
u double prime over u prime

00:56:39.730 --> 00:56:41.003 align:middle line:90%
is equal to--

00:56:41.003 --> 00:56:44.950 align:middle line:90%


00:56:44.950 --> 00:56:45.580 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:56:45.580 --> 00:56:46.900 align:middle line:90%
Have I done this right?

00:56:46.900 --> 00:56:50.980 align:middle line:84%
u double prime
divided by u prime

00:56:50.980 --> 00:56:55.155 align:middle line:84%
is equal to minus 2
mu over sigma squared.

00:56:55.155 --> 00:57:02.470 align:middle line:90%


00:57:02.470 --> 00:57:13.210 align:middle line:84%
And so this is the derivative
is equal to the derivative

00:57:13.210 --> 00:57:15.240 align:middle line:90%
of the log of u prime.

00:57:15.240 --> 00:57:18.400 align:middle line:90%


00:57:18.400 --> 00:57:25.840 align:middle line:84%
And so if we integrate this,
we basically get log of u prime

00:57:25.840 --> 00:57:35.963 align:middle line:84%
is equal to minus 2 mu over
sigma squared x plus a constant.

00:57:35.963 --> 00:57:39.010 align:middle line:90%


00:57:39.010 --> 00:57:44.230 align:middle line:84%
And this is true if
mu is not equal to 0.

00:57:44.230 --> 00:57:46.750 align:middle line:84%
And so we basically
get that u prime

00:57:46.750 --> 00:57:50.920 align:middle line:84%
is equal to e to the
minus 2 mu x over sigma

00:57:50.920 --> 00:57:53.023 align:middle line:90%
squared times a constant.

00:57:53.023 --> 00:57:58.960 align:middle line:90%


00:57:58.960 --> 00:58:03.560 align:middle line:84%
And what if we integrate
both sides of this?

00:58:03.560 --> 00:58:05.560 align:middle line:90%
We then get u.

00:58:05.560 --> 00:58:08.560 align:middle line:84%
So what function
has a derivative

00:58:08.560 --> 00:58:10.076 align:middle line:90%
equal to an exponential?

00:58:10.076 --> 00:58:12.817 align:middle line:90%


00:58:12.817 --> 00:58:13.900 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The exponential.

00:58:13.900 --> 00:58:15.970 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Just
the exponential, yeah.

00:58:15.970 --> 00:58:18.760 align:middle line:84%
So we get u is
just going to equal

00:58:18.760 --> 00:58:23.925 align:middle line:84%
a constant times e to the minus
2 mu over sigma squared x.

00:58:23.925 --> 00:58:26.710 align:middle line:90%


00:58:26.710 --> 00:58:36.370 align:middle line:84%
And if mu is equal
to 0, then we have

00:58:36.370 --> 00:58:47.316 align:middle line:84%
the log of u prime is equal
to 0, or the derivative--

00:58:47.316 --> 00:58:52.490 align:middle line:90%


00:58:52.490 --> 00:58:55.120 align:middle line:84%
yeah, we basically have the
derivative of the log of u

00:58:55.120 --> 00:58:58.940 align:middle line:90%
prime is equal to 0.

00:58:58.940 --> 00:59:02.295 align:middle line:90%
So we end up getting--

00:59:02.295 --> 00:59:04.960 align:middle line:90%


00:59:04.960 --> 00:59:07.550 align:middle line:84%
let's see-- basically a linear
function for the solution.

00:59:07.550 --> 00:59:09.940 align:middle line:90%
So let me go to the notes here.

00:59:09.940 --> 00:59:13.590 align:middle line:84%
Well, we basically get
u is equal to Ax plus B,

00:59:13.590 --> 00:59:20.660 align:middle line:84%
if mu is equal to 0, and it's
equal to this constant, A times

00:59:20.660 --> 00:59:23.490 align:middle line:84%
e to the minus 2 mu
x over sigma squared.

00:59:23.490 --> 00:59:24.150 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:59:24.150 --> 00:59:27.770 align:middle line:84%
Well, this general
solution has to satisfy

00:59:27.770 --> 00:59:29.420 align:middle line:90%
the initial conditions.

00:59:29.420 --> 00:59:35.150 align:middle line:84%
u of a is going to equal 0,
and u of b is going to equal 1.

00:59:35.150 --> 00:59:40.870 align:middle line:90%
So if we start at a, where the--

00:59:40.870 --> 00:59:44.660 align:middle line:84%
basically, we stop at
a, if we start at a.

00:59:44.660 --> 00:59:47.760 align:middle line:90%
So u of 0 is going to equal 0.

00:59:47.760 --> 00:59:53.120 align:middle line:84%
And if we start at
b, it's equal to 1.

00:59:53.120 --> 00:59:59.030 align:middle line:84%
We end up getting this
formula for what u is.

00:59:59.030 --> 01:00:04.260 align:middle line:84%
And so what's
interesting to ask is,

01:00:04.260 --> 01:00:11.720 align:middle line:84%
how does this probability
vary with different drifts

01:00:11.720 --> 01:00:13.665 align:middle line:90%
and volatilities?

01:00:13.665 --> 01:00:14.165 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:00:14.165 --> 01:00:20.300 align:middle line:84%
So here's the graph
of these probabilities

01:00:20.300 --> 01:00:26.430 align:middle line:90%
of hitting b before a when x--

01:00:26.430 --> 01:00:30.680 align:middle line:90%
well, when a is 0.

01:00:30.680 --> 01:00:39.890 align:middle line:84%
So if we have 0 here
and b is equal to 3,

01:00:39.890 --> 01:00:50.030 align:middle line:84%
then here's the initial
value x between 0 and 3.

01:00:50.030 --> 01:00:55.840 align:middle line:84%
And we have the probabilities
of hitting b before a

01:00:55.840 --> 01:01:01.850 align:middle line:84%
And if our mu is very
high, so the trend

01:01:01.850 --> 01:01:05.660 align:middle line:90%
is up in the Brownian motion--

01:01:05.660 --> 01:01:11.420 align:middle line:84%
so basically, if mu is
greater than 0, then

01:01:11.420 --> 01:01:17.070 align:middle line:84%
we basically have an
upward-trending path on average,

01:01:17.070 --> 01:01:21.260 align:middle line:84%
and we get high probabilities
of hitting b first.

01:01:21.260 --> 01:01:30.960 align:middle line:84%
And as we decrease the
drift from 3 down to 0.5,

01:01:30.960 --> 01:01:33.200 align:middle line:90%
we get this Brown curve.

01:01:33.200 --> 01:01:37.790 align:middle line:84%
And the limiting case,
where mu is equal to 0,

01:01:37.790 --> 01:01:46.460 align:middle line:84%
so there's no drift at all,
corresponds to the probability

01:01:46.460 --> 01:01:48.650 align:middle line:90%
given by this line.

01:01:48.650 --> 01:01:54.410 align:middle line:84%
And so given the starting
value x, the closer you

01:01:54.410 --> 01:02:00.530 align:middle line:84%
are to b, the more likely you
will be to hit b first and vice

01:02:00.530 --> 01:02:02.660 align:middle line:90%
versa with a.

01:02:02.660 --> 01:02:10.000 align:middle line:84%
So these probabilities vary with
the different model parameters.

01:02:10.000 --> 01:02:16.290 align:middle line:84%
Here is the corresponding graph
when there's a negative drift.

01:02:16.290 --> 01:02:26.840 align:middle line:84%
So the likelihood
of hitting b first

01:02:26.840 --> 01:02:33.780 align:middle line:84%
is proportional when
the drift term is 0--

01:02:33.780 --> 01:02:36.640 align:middle line:84%
basically how
close you are to b.

01:02:36.640 --> 01:02:44.440 align:middle line:84%
But as we increase the
magnitude of the negative drift,

01:02:44.440 --> 01:02:48.360 align:middle line:84%
we basically get lower and
lower probabilities coming out.

01:02:48.360 --> 01:02:51.150 align:middle line:90%
So that's that solution.

01:02:51.150 --> 01:02:51.650 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:02:51.650 --> 01:02:53.580 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

01:02:53.580 --> 01:02:56.610 align:middle line:84%
The next comment
about Brownian motion

01:02:56.610 --> 01:03:02.340 align:middle line:84%
is a very important property
that the Brownian motion process

01:03:02.340 --> 01:03:04.560 align:middle line:90%
is not differentiable.

01:03:04.560 --> 01:03:07.060 align:middle line:84%
So when you look at a
Brownian motion path,

01:03:07.060 --> 01:03:08.680 align:middle line:90%
it looks very jagged.

01:03:08.680 --> 01:03:13.740 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, that jaggedness
is characteristic of there

01:03:13.740 --> 01:03:17.880 align:middle line:84%
not being a smooth derivative,
or it's not being smooth enough

01:03:17.880 --> 01:03:19.540 align:middle line:90%
to compute a derivative.

01:03:19.540 --> 01:03:23.850 align:middle line:84%
And so if we look at the
increment of the Brownian motion

01:03:23.850 --> 01:03:27.060 align:middle line:84%
from t to t plus
delta t and have

01:03:27.060 --> 01:03:32.310 align:middle line:84%
that being normal with mean
0 and variance delta t,

01:03:32.310 --> 01:03:34.080 align:middle line:90%
the length of that increment--

01:03:34.080 --> 01:03:39.520 align:middle line:84%
if we normalize the
increment by delta t--

01:03:39.520 --> 01:03:41.160 align:middle line:84%
so we're basically
trying to look

01:03:41.160 --> 01:03:47.520 align:middle line:84%
at the slope between
t and t plus delta t--

01:03:47.520 --> 01:03:52.380 align:middle line:84%
then that has a normal
distribution with mean 0,

01:03:52.380 --> 01:03:56.190 align:middle line:90%
but variance 1 over delta t.

01:03:56.190 --> 01:04:02.430 align:middle line:84%
So this normalized
increment is going to not be

01:04:02.430 --> 01:04:04.420 align:middle line:90%
well defined in the limit.

01:04:04.420 --> 01:04:06.930 align:middle line:84%
It's basically going
to be equivalent to--

01:04:06.930 --> 01:04:11.230 align:middle line:84%
or the limiting value
basically doesn't exist.

01:04:11.230 --> 01:04:15.050 align:middle line:84%
It basically becomes an
explosive distribution.

01:04:15.050 --> 01:04:22.470 align:middle line:84%
So that's a very important
property of Brownian motion.

01:04:22.470 --> 01:04:27.000 align:middle line:84%
Something that is a positive
property of Brownian motion

01:04:27.000 --> 01:04:32.580 align:middle line:84%
is the issue of
quadratic variation.

01:04:32.580 --> 01:04:37.740 align:middle line:84%
And so to define
quadratic variation,

01:04:37.740 --> 01:04:44.653 align:middle line:84%
we consider looking at
partitions of the time.

01:04:44.653 --> 01:04:49.470 align:middle line:90%


01:04:49.470 --> 01:04:58.620 align:middle line:84%
So we have pi, which is, what, a
partition of the interval from 0

01:04:58.620 --> 01:05:00.990 align:middle line:90%
to t, say--

01:05:00.990 --> 01:05:04.960 align:middle line:90%
or 0 to capital T.

01:05:04.960 --> 01:05:12.100 align:middle line:84%
So we have a time scale
finishing at T, starting at 0.

01:05:12.100 --> 01:05:18.190 align:middle line:84%
And we can just draw arbitrary
points t, j, j equaling 1 up

01:05:18.190 --> 01:05:32.620 align:middle line:84%
to n, say, or 1 to n minus 1,
that are inside this interval.

01:05:32.620 --> 01:05:33.893 align:middle line:90%
And they're ordered.

01:05:33.893 --> 01:05:37.780 align:middle line:90%


01:05:37.780 --> 01:05:45.700 align:middle line:84%
If we consider the partition
corresponding to these time

01:05:45.700 --> 01:05:50.500 align:middle line:84%
points, we can
define a magnitude

01:05:50.500 --> 01:06:02.590 align:middle line:84%
of the partition equal to the
maximum of tj minus tj minus 1

01:06:02.590 --> 01:06:03.585 align:middle line:90%
over j.

01:06:03.585 --> 01:06:08.500 align:middle line:90%


01:06:08.500 --> 01:06:12.910 align:middle line:84%
And then we can talk about
the quadratic variation

01:06:12.910 --> 01:06:18.130 align:middle line:84%
of the process, which
is to look at increments

01:06:18.130 --> 01:06:26.200 align:middle line:84%
of b over these intervals
from between tj minus 1

01:06:26.200 --> 01:06:33.700 align:middle line:84%
and tj, square those, and sum
the squared of those values.

01:06:33.700 --> 01:06:42.640 align:middle line:84%
This is an appropriate term
for a quadratic variation.

01:06:42.640 --> 01:06:57.340 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider the limit
as the partition width gets

01:06:57.340 --> 01:07:01.180 align:middle line:84%
narrower and
narrower, then we'll

01:07:01.180 --> 01:07:06.460 align:middle line:84%
define this limiting value
to be the quadratic variation

01:07:06.460 --> 01:07:10.000 align:middle line:90%
of the Brownian motion process.

01:07:10.000 --> 01:07:13.480 align:middle line:84%
So we can define the quadratic
variation corresponding

01:07:13.480 --> 01:07:18.440 align:middle line:84%
to a given partition and then
the quadratic variation overall,

01:07:18.440 --> 01:07:20.680 align:middle line:84%
if we take the limit
over partitions that are

01:07:20.680 --> 01:07:24.730 align:middle line:90%
getting infinitesimally small.

01:07:24.730 --> 01:07:30.340 align:middle line:84%
And the claim is that
this quadratic variation

01:07:30.340 --> 01:07:36.430 align:middle line:84%
is going to equal capital
T, the length of the period,

01:07:36.430 --> 01:07:40.270 align:middle line:90%
with probability 1.

01:07:40.270 --> 01:07:41.990 align:middle line:90%
So what does that mean?

01:07:41.990 --> 01:07:50.560 align:middle line:84%
That means that for
every realization

01:07:50.560 --> 01:07:56.870 align:middle line:84%
of the process, or
almost every realization,

01:07:56.870 --> 01:08:03.200 align:middle line:84%
this quadratic variation is
going to equal a constant.

01:08:03.200 --> 01:08:12.440 align:middle line:84%
So here, I'm looking at
examples of random walks

01:08:12.440 --> 01:08:20.550 align:middle line:84%
that are normalized to
be between time 0 and 1.

01:08:20.550 --> 01:08:23.090 align:middle line:84%
So these are the
normalized random walks,

01:08:23.090 --> 01:08:25.910 align:middle line:84%
which are effectively
Brownian-- or whose

01:08:25.910 --> 01:08:28.189 align:middle line:90%
limits are Brownian motion.

01:08:28.189 --> 01:08:30.319 align:middle line:84%
And in the top
panel, I'm drawing

01:08:30.319 --> 01:08:35.850 align:middle line:84%
the path of the random
walk, with Gaussian steps.

01:08:35.850 --> 01:08:39.380 align:middle line:84%
And in the bottom, I'm drawing
the cumulative quadratic

01:08:39.380 --> 01:08:42.350 align:middle line:90%
variation.

01:08:42.350 --> 01:08:46.399 align:middle line:84%
So this is up to 20,
30, 40 steps now.

01:08:46.399 --> 01:08:55.100 align:middle line:84%
As we increase to 100 steps,
this cumulative quadratic

01:08:55.100 --> 01:09:02.479 align:middle line:84%
variation is getting
pretty close to the time t

01:09:02.479 --> 01:09:06.180 align:middle line:84%
over which we're accumulating
the quadratic variation.

01:09:06.180 --> 01:09:17.880 align:middle line:84%
And as we increase the number
of steps from 200 to many more--

01:09:17.880 --> 01:09:21.439 align:middle line:90%
1,000 and then 2,000--

01:09:21.439 --> 01:09:24.920 align:middle line:84%
you can see that, basically,
the quadratic variation

01:09:24.920 --> 01:09:29.510 align:middle line:84%
of the process
cumulates, basically,

01:09:29.510 --> 01:09:34.460 align:middle line:84%
to the time period over
which that's being cumulated.

01:09:34.460 --> 01:09:40.319 align:middle line:84%
So this property is a
really nice property.

01:09:40.319 --> 01:09:43.760 align:middle line:84%
What it highlights
is that there are

01:09:43.760 --> 01:09:49.550 align:middle line:84%
some significant regularities
to Brownian motion.

01:09:49.550 --> 01:09:58.970 align:middle line:84%
And the regularity here is that
the variation of the increments

01:09:58.970 --> 01:10:03.710 align:middle line:84%
normalizes, basically, to
the length of the period

01:10:03.710 --> 01:10:05.220 align:middle line:90%
over which they're accumulated.

01:10:05.220 --> 01:10:10.130 align:middle line:84%
So the quadratic variation is
proportional to the time period.

01:10:10.130 --> 01:10:15.440 align:middle line:84%
Now, this idea, or this
property of Brownian motion,

01:10:15.440 --> 01:10:21.440 align:middle line:84%
is one that can be
explored with time series

01:10:21.440 --> 01:10:26.150 align:middle line:84%
that we might think are
well-modeled by Brownian motion.

01:10:26.150 --> 01:10:40.940 align:middle line:84%
So if we were to look at a time
series of the log of Pt divided

01:10:40.940 --> 01:10:55.230 align:middle line:84%
by P0 for Pt equal to
0, 1, up to capital N,

01:10:55.230 --> 01:11:00.040 align:middle line:84%
basically, for prices of
assets and equity markets,

01:11:00.040 --> 01:11:07.206 align:middle line:84%
this corresponds to the log
return from time 0 to t.

01:11:07.206 --> 01:11:16.050 align:middle line:84%
And that log return from 0 to t
might follow a Brownian motion

01:11:16.050 --> 01:11:22.570 align:middle line:84%
process with drift,
positive return.

01:11:22.570 --> 01:11:29.350 align:middle line:84%
And so we basically can
have this process evolving.

01:11:29.350 --> 01:11:35.310 align:middle line:84%
If we were to calculate
the quadratic variation

01:11:35.310 --> 01:11:41.276 align:middle line:84%
of this process,
then if it followed--

01:11:41.276 --> 01:11:44.760 align:middle line:90%


01:11:44.760 --> 01:11:47.670 align:middle line:84%
if it followed a
Brownian motion process,

01:11:47.670 --> 01:11:52.710 align:middle line:84%
it would tend to grow
according to a straight line,

01:11:52.710 --> 01:11:59.460 align:middle line:84%
with slope equal
to sigma squared.

01:11:59.460 --> 01:12:04.650 align:middle line:84%
And in empirical
modeling, if we look

01:12:04.650 --> 01:12:08.220 align:middle line:84%
at this quadratic
variation of an asset

01:12:08.220 --> 01:12:13.890 align:middle line:84%
and see that it's
not a straight line--

01:12:13.890 --> 01:12:16.950 align:middle line:84%
there may be periods
where the slope is low

01:12:16.950 --> 01:12:22.200 align:middle line:84%
and periods where the slope is
higher and it curves around--

01:12:22.200 --> 01:12:26.700 align:middle line:84%
a way of trying to model
the dynamic volatility

01:12:26.700 --> 01:12:35.700 align:middle line:84%
could be suggested by studying
these plots and using them.

01:12:35.700 --> 01:12:40.380 align:middle line:84%
So it's a very
interesting property.

01:12:40.380 --> 01:12:43.530 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see.

01:12:43.530 --> 01:12:46.470 align:middle line:90%


01:12:46.470 --> 01:12:47.200 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

01:12:47.200 --> 01:12:52.420 align:middle line:84%
There's one last note in
the slide in the notes.

01:12:52.420 --> 01:13:03.690 align:middle line:84%
But before doing that,
I wanted to highlight

01:13:03.690 --> 01:13:12.310 align:middle line:84%
a connection of Brownian motion
to the Laplace distribution.

01:13:12.310 --> 01:13:17.736 align:middle line:90%
And so if we have--

01:13:17.736 --> 01:13:18.750 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

01:13:18.750 --> 01:13:28.710 align:middle line:84%
If we have B of t, which
is Brownian motion,

01:13:28.710 --> 01:13:35.323 align:middle line:90%
and we observe it at--

01:13:35.323 --> 01:13:38.190 align:middle line:90%


01:13:38.190 --> 01:13:42.413 align:middle line:84%
let's see-- if we look
at, basically, x of--

01:13:42.413 --> 01:13:48.130 align:middle line:90%


01:13:48.130 --> 01:13:55.510 align:middle line:84%
if we observe it at
times t equals 0, 1, 2,

01:13:55.510 --> 01:14:05.890 align:middle line:84%
discrete times, then
the delta B of t

01:14:05.890 --> 01:14:11.770 align:middle line:84%
are distributed as
normal with mean--

01:14:11.770 --> 01:14:19.790 align:middle line:84%
I guess it's mu t and variance
sigma squared times 1,

01:14:19.790 --> 01:14:21.910 align:middle line:90%
which is delta t.

01:14:21.910 --> 01:14:23.280 align:middle line:90%
And these are iid.

01:14:23.280 --> 01:14:26.890 align:middle line:90%


01:14:26.890 --> 01:14:35.110 align:middle line:84%
So we have, basically, time
points 0, 1, 2, and so forth.

01:14:35.110 --> 01:14:37.960 align:middle line:84%
If instead of
observing the process

01:14:37.960 --> 01:14:42.820 align:middle line:84%
at equal increments
of time, let's

01:14:42.820 --> 01:14:48.140 align:middle line:84%
consider the possibility that
the time increments are random.

01:14:48.140 --> 01:15:05.240 align:middle line:84%
So let's consider B of t for
t equal to T1, T2, up to Tn,

01:15:05.240 --> 01:15:10.790 align:middle line:84%
where the delta T's,
the increments of time,

01:15:10.790 --> 01:15:15.710 align:middle line:84%
are distributed as an
exponential random variable,

01:15:15.710 --> 01:15:19.280 align:middle line:90%
with some mean lambda, say.

01:15:19.280 --> 01:15:24.750 align:middle line:84%
So we're not observing the
process at regular intervals.

01:15:24.750 --> 01:15:28.640 align:middle line:84%
We're observing it
at random intervals.

01:15:28.640 --> 01:15:34.580 align:middle line:84%
It turns out, then,
that the Brownian motion

01:15:34.580 --> 01:15:44.090 align:middle line:84%
process has increments that
are Laplace distributed.

01:15:44.090 --> 01:15:50.710 align:middle line:84%
So the delta B of t,
then will, in fact,

01:15:50.710 --> 01:15:55.240 align:middle line:84%
have what's called a
Laplace distribution.

01:15:55.240 --> 01:16:00.940 align:middle line:84%
And so instead of having
a bell-shaped curve, which

01:16:00.940 --> 01:16:03.850 align:middle line:84%
is a Gaussian
distribution, it'll

01:16:03.850 --> 01:16:10.990 align:middle line:84%
be a bilateral, exponential
distribution about the mean.

01:16:10.990 --> 01:16:18.350 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that with
Standard and Poor's 500 returns,

01:16:18.350 --> 01:16:24.040 align:middle line:84%
this Laplace model seems to
fit the data much better, which

01:16:24.040 --> 01:16:29.500 align:middle line:84%
leads to the consideration of
what are called subordinated

01:16:29.500 --> 01:16:33.520 align:middle line:84%
stochastic processes,
where the time scale is

01:16:33.520 --> 01:16:37.600 align:middle line:90%
different than our clock time.

01:16:37.600 --> 01:16:38.360 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:16:38.360 --> 01:16:39.340 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's see.

01:16:39.340 --> 01:16:44.480 align:middle line:84%
The final slide here for today
addresses what are called

01:16:44.480 --> 01:16:46.500 align:middle line:90%
adapted processes.

01:16:46.500 --> 01:16:53.030 align:middle line:84%
And so in financial
modeling, we can

01:16:53.030 --> 01:16:59.420 align:middle line:84%
think of there being a
stochastic process X of t

01:16:59.420 --> 01:17:04.670 align:middle line:84%
characterizing relevant
dynamics in the system.

01:17:04.670 --> 01:17:09.110 align:middle line:84%
And we can think
of X representing

01:17:09.110 --> 01:17:12.320 align:middle line:84%
the prices of a single asset
or a collection of assets

01:17:12.320 --> 01:17:14.220 align:middle line:90%
as it varies over time.

01:17:14.220 --> 01:17:20.570 align:middle line:84%
Then, as we let
time increase, there

01:17:20.570 --> 01:17:27.560 align:middle line:84%
are events that relate to
what has happened so far.

01:17:27.560 --> 01:17:34.010 align:middle line:84%
And we can think of
these script F sub t

01:17:34.010 --> 01:17:38.540 align:middle line:84%
to be the information
on realizations

01:17:38.540 --> 01:17:40.910 align:middle line:90%
up to time little t.

01:17:40.910 --> 01:17:48.500 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider script
F sub t prime with t

01:17:48.500 --> 01:17:51.440 align:middle line:84%
prime greater than t,
then that corresponds

01:17:51.440 --> 01:17:54.710 align:middle line:84%
to information about
all realizations from t

01:17:54.710 --> 01:17:56.070 align:middle line:90%
up until t prime.

01:17:56.070 --> 01:17:58.370 align:middle line:84%
So it's giving us
more information

01:17:58.370 --> 01:18:01.340 align:middle line:84%
about what's happened
with the process.

01:18:01.340 --> 01:18:12.860 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we want to look at the
stochastic modeling of a process

01:18:12.860 --> 01:18:19.910 align:middle line:84%
Y that depends on trading
on assets with dynamics X,

01:18:19.910 --> 01:18:27.530 align:middle line:84%
then we want our
process Y to be adapted

01:18:27.530 --> 01:18:33.570 align:middle line:84%
to X, which essentially means we
can't see the future in trading.

01:18:33.570 --> 01:18:40.040 align:middle line:84%
And so our Y of t, which is
perhaps the value of trading up

01:18:40.040 --> 01:18:46.340 align:middle line:84%
until time t, is a
function only of the values

01:18:46.340 --> 01:18:49.790 align:middle line:90%
of the process prior to time t.

01:18:49.790 --> 01:18:55.040 align:middle line:84%
And so we consider
transformations

01:18:55.040 --> 01:19:02.270 align:middle line:84%
of our original process to the
process of, say, trading gains

01:19:02.270 --> 01:19:05.640 align:middle line:84%
and losses, according to
some trading strategy.

01:19:05.640 --> 01:19:13.110 align:middle line:84%
And these will be well defined
if the process is adapted.

01:19:13.110 --> 01:19:20.360 align:middle line:84%
So with that, the topics
connect with what we had

01:19:20.360 --> 01:19:23.330 align:middle line:84%
in our previous
lecture, which was

01:19:23.330 --> 01:19:29.180 align:middle line:84%
we can have martingales
defined on X,

01:19:29.180 --> 01:19:32.840 align:middle line:84%
and then we can define
martingales relating

01:19:32.840 --> 01:19:43.360 align:middle line:84%
to Y when we combine
adaptive functions to X.

01:19:43.360 --> 01:19:48.150 align:middle line:84%
And so the martingale theory
extends to modeling trading

01:19:48.150 --> 01:19:50.200 align:middle line:90%
strategies, effectively.

01:19:50.200 --> 01:19:53.730 align:middle line:84%
And what's interesting
with those techniques

01:19:53.730 --> 01:19:59.430 align:middle line:84%
is that you end up
transforming the process

01:19:59.430 --> 01:20:02.160 align:middle line:90%
to have a constant mean.

01:20:02.160 --> 01:20:05.350 align:middle line:84%
Martingales are processes
with a constant mean.

01:20:05.350 --> 01:20:12.840 align:middle line:84%
And so those arguments are
applied again and again

01:20:12.840 --> 01:20:14.920 align:middle line:90%
in solving for their properties.

01:20:14.920 --> 01:20:24.480 align:middle line:84%
And option pricing can be
expressed in this context

01:20:24.480 --> 01:20:25.660 align:middle line:90%
as well.

01:20:25.660 --> 01:20:26.500 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:20:26.500 --> 01:20:28.990 align:middle line:90%
We'll finish there for today.

01:20:28.990 --> 01:20:35.000 align:middle line:90%